# Room Name - TryHackMe: ItsyBitsy
Date: 05 October 2026
Difficulty: Medium
Category: SOC Level 1, ELK, network log analysis

# Scenario
During routine SOC monitoring, an IDS alert flagged potential C2 communication from a machine belonging to Browne, a user in the HR department. A suspicious file containing a pattern in the format THM{...} was reportedly accessed. Due to limited resources, only a week of HTTP connection logs were collected and ingested into the connection_logs index in Kibana. The goal was to investigate the user's network activity, identify the C2 link, and recover the contents of the accessed file.

# Tools Used
- Kibana (Discover)
- Elasticsearch with KQL
- Web browser

# What I Did
- Logged into Kibana and opened the Discover tab with the connection_logs index selected.
- Set the time range to 1 March 2022 to 31 March 2022 to scope the investigation to the relevant period.
- Reviewed the source_ip field in the available fields panel. Two IPs were present, and one accounted for only 0.4% of events, making it the outlier worth investigating.
- Filtered on the suspicious source IP to isolate its activity.

```
source_ip : 192.166.65.54
```

- Reviewed the two returned events and identified the user agent, destination IP, host, and URI.
- Pivoted on the destination IP to confirm no other hosts in the logs had connected to it.

```
destination_ip : 104.23.99.190
```

- Combined the host and URI fields to build the full C2 URL and visited it in a browser to identify the file name and its contents.

# What I Found
- 1,482 events were recorded in the connection_logs index for March 2022.
- The suspected user's machine was identified by the IP 192.166.65.54, which appeared in only 0.4% of events.
- On 10 March 2022 at 11:23:11, the host made two HTTP requests (a HEAD followed by a GET) to 104.23.99.190 on port 80.
- The user agent for both requests was bitsadmin, a legitimate Windows binary abused to download files.
- The destination host was pastebin.com, a public file-sharing site used here as a C2 channel.
- The full C2 URL was pastebin.com/yTg0Ah6a.
- The file accessed on the paste was named secret.txt.
- The file contained the secret code THM{SECRET__CODE}.

# Key Learning
- Outlier analysis on field value distributions in Kibana is a fast way to spot a single suspicious host among large volumes of normal traffic.
- Living off the land binaries (LOLBins) such as bitsadmin are commonly abused because they are signed Microsoft tools and often bypass basic allowlisting.
- Legitimate public services like Pastebin are frequently used for C2 and payload staging, since traffic to them blends in with normal browsing.
- A HEAD request followed immediately by a GET to the same URI is a typical pattern of BITS jobs checking a resource before downloading it.
- Pivoting from one indicator (source IP) to another (destination IP, host, URI) helps build a complete picture of an incident from limited log data.

# What I Would Flag in a Real SOC
- Any HTTP request with a bitsadmin user agent originating from a standard user workstation, especially in departments like HR where it has no business use.
- Connections from internal hosts to paste sites such as pastebin.com, particularly raw content retrieval over plain HTTP.
- Isolate the affected host 192.166.65.54 and escalate for endpoint investigation to check for downloaded payloads, BITS jobs, and persistence.
- Block the C2 URL pastebin.com/yTg0Ah6a and consider restricting access to paste sites at the proxy level.
- Hunt across the environment for other hosts using bitsadmin or contacting 104.23.99.190 to determine the scope of compromise.
